  <data name="groups_move" xml:space="preserve"><value>Move to Group</value><comment>A menu within a tab&apos;s own menu (on a Mac in the File menu, for the tab in front), and on Android the heading over the same choices in the tab&apos;s menu: the Groups of the row by name, the one the tab is in marked. Choosing one puts the tab in that Group; a tab that is no Site is kept as one by it.</comment></data>
  <data name="groups_new" xml:space="preserve"><value>New Group…</value><comment>In &apos;Move to Group&apos;: opens the page that makes a Group, and puts the tab in it.</comment></data>
  <data name="groups_none" xml:space="preserve"><value>No Group</value><comment>In &apos;Move to Group&apos;, after the Groups: the choice for a tab that is in none. Chosen for a Site that is in a Group, it takes it out; it stays a Site.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear" xml:space="preserve"><value>Clear…</value><comment>Button in the History list that asks which visits to forget.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear_cancel" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cancel</value><comment>Choice: leave history as it is.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear_detail" xml:space="preserve"><value>Visits that are forgotten can’t be brought back.</value><comment>Under the question of which visits to forget.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear_everything" xml:space="preserve"><value>Everything</value><comment>Choice: forget every visit.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear_hour" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Last Hour</value><comment>Choice: forget the visits of the last hour.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear_title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Forget which visits?</value><comment>Question asked by the Clear button of the History list, above the choices.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_clear_today" xml:space="preserve"><value>Today</value><comment>Choice: forget the visits since midnight.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_done" xml:space="preserve"><value>Done</value><comment>Button that closes the History list.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_empty" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pages you visit will be listed here.</value><comment>Shown in the History list when nothing has been visited yet.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_forget" xml:space="preserve"><value>Forget This Visit</value><comment>Accessibility label of the button beside a visit that removes it from history.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_none_found" xml:space="preserve"><value>No visits match.</value><comment>Shown in the History list when the words typed find no visit.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_older" xml:space="preserve"><value>Show Older Visits</value><comment>Button at the end of the History list that shows the next, older page of visits.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_open" xml:space="preserve"><value>History and Downloads</value><comment>Label of the button, beside the tabs, that opens the lists of pages visited and of files downloaded.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_search" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search history</value><comment>Placeholder and accessibility label of the field for finding a visit by words of its title or address.</comment></data>
  <data name="history_title" xml:space="preserve"><value>History</value><comment>Title of the list of pages visited.</comment></data>
  <data name="layout_side" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tabs at the Side</value><comment>Command in the Mac&apos;s View menu (Control-Command-S) while the window&apos;s tabs are along its top: they are drawn down its side instead, in a sidebar. Nothing else changes.</comment></data>
  <data name="layout_top" xml:space="preserve"><value>Tabs at the Top</value><comment>The same command while the tabs are down the side: they are drawn along the top again.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_cancel" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cancel</value><comment>In a link&apos;s menu: close the menu and do nothing.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_copy" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy Link</value><comment>In a link&apos;s menu: copy the link&apos;s address.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_download" xml:space="preserve"><value>Download Linked File</value><comment>In a link&apos;s menu: save what the link leads to as a file, with the person&apos;s downloads.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_download_image" xml:space="preserve"><value>Download Image</value><comment>In a picture&apos;s menu: save the picture as a file, with the person&apos;s downloads.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_image_menu" xml:space="preserve"><value>Image</value><comment>Accessibility name of the menu shown for a picture of a page that was pressed and held.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_menu" xml:space="preserve"><value>Link</value><comment>Accessibility name of the menu shown for a link of a page that was pressed and held.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_open_image_new_tab" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Image in New Tab</value><comment>In a picture&apos;s menu (on a Mac beside the system&apos;s own items such as Copy Image): open the picture by itself in a new tab.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_open_link_new_tab" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open Link in New Tab</value><comment>In the menu a Mac shows for a link (right-click), beside the system&apos;s own items such as Copy Link: open the link in a new tab.</comment></data>
  <data name="link_open_new_tab" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open in New Tab</value><comment>In a link&apos;s menu: open the link in a new tab, behind the one being read.</comment></data>
  <data name="menu_close_window" xml:space="preserve"><value>Close Window</value><comment>Command in the Mac&apos;s File menu (Shift-Command-W): close the window, however many tabs it has.</comment></data>
  <data name="menu_history" xml:space="preserve"><value>History</value><comment>Name of a menu in the Mac&apos;s menu bar: going back and forward, and the list of pages visited.</comment></data>
  <data name="outside_search" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search with WebTiger</value><comment>Android: the item WebTiger adds to other apps&apos; menu of selected text. Choosing it opens the words as a search in WebTiger, with the search engine the person chose. Short: it sits in a small bar.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_clear" xml:space="preserve"><value>Remove All</value><comment>Button in the Permissions list that takes back what every site was allowed. Each will ask again.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_done" xml:space="preserve"><value>Done</value><comment>Button that closes the Permissions list.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_empty" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sites you allow to download, or to use your camera, microphone or location, will be listed here.</value><comment>Shown in the Permissions list when no site was allowed anything.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_forget" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stop Allowing</value><comment>Accessibility label of the button beside a site in the Permissions list, which takes back what it was allowed. The site will ask again.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_kind_camera" xml:space="preserve"><value>Camera</value><comment>What a site was allowed to use, under its name in the Permissions list. Several are joined as a list by the system.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_kind_downloads" xml:space="preserve"><value>Downloads</value><comment>What a site was allowed to do, under its name in the Permissions list: download files without asking each time.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_kind_location" xml:space="preserve"><value>Location</value><comment>What a site was allowed to use, under its name in the Permissions list: where the device is.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_kind_microphone" xml:space="preserve"><value>Microphone</value><comment>What a site was allowed to use, under its name in the Permissions list.</comment></data>
  <data name="permissions_title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Permissions</value><comment>Title of the list of sites that were allowed to download, or to use the camera, the microphone or the location, and the name of its part of the switch beside History and Downloads.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_back" xml:space="preserve"><value>Back to {0}</value><comment>Button on the page behind a private window&apos;s chip on a phone: shows the other window again and leaves the private one open. %@ is the name of the Space the other window shows.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_chip" xml:space="preserve"><value>Private</value><comment>Chip in the strip of tabs of a private window, where an ordinary window names its Space. Short: on a narrow strip only its first letter is shown.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_close" xml:space="preserve"><value>Close Private Window</value><comment>Button on the page behind a private window&apos;s chip, and command: closes the private window, and everything of it is gone.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_hides" xml:space="preserve"><value>It does not hide you from the sites you visit, or from your network.</value><comment>What a private window does not do: follows the sentence that says what it means.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_keeps" xml:space="preserve"><value>Nothing you visit in this window is kept on this device: no history, and nothing a site stored once the window is closed. Files you save stay.</value><comment>What a private window means: in an empty tab of one, and on the page behind its chip.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_new" xml:space="preserve"><value>New Private Window</value><comment>Command in the Mac&apos;s File menu (Shift-Command-N): open a private window.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_row_detail" xml:space="preserve"><value>Nothing it visits is kept on this device.</value><comment>Under &apos;Private Window&apos; in the list of Spaces.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_row_open" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open</value><comment>Said beside &apos;Private Window&apos; in the list of Spaces when a private window is open already: the row then shows it.</comment></data>
  <data name="private_window" xml:space="preserve"><value>Private Window</value><comment>Name of a private window: the last row of the list of Spaces on a phone (it opens one), the heading of the page that explains it, and what a screen reader says for the chip.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_allow" xml:space="preserve"><value>Allow</value><comment>Button in a tab&apos;s prompt: lets the site use what it asked for, now and when it asks again.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_answer" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your answer</value><comment>Accessibility label of the field in the bar where a page asks for a line of text.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_cancel" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cancel</value><comment>Button in the bar that shows what a page asks: refuses it.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_download" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} wants to download “{1}”.</value><comment>The sentence in the bar a tab shows when a page starts a download and its site was not yet allowed to. %1$@ is the site, as the address field shows it; %2$@ is the name the file would be saved under, in quotation marks so that where it begins and ends can be seen.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_kind_camera" xml:space="preserve"><value>your camera</value><comment>What a page asks to use, in the prompt.permission sentence.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_kind_location" xml:space="preserve"><value>your location</value><comment>What a page asks to use, in the prompt.permission sentence: where the device is.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_kind_microphone" xml:space="preserve"><value>your microphone</value><comment>What a page asks to use, in the prompt.permission sentence.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_ok" xml:space="preserve"><value>OK</value><comment>Button in the bar that shows what a page says or asks: accepts it.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_permission" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} wants to use {1}.</value><comment>The sentence in the bar a tab shows when a page asks to use something of the device&apos;s. %1$@ is the site, as the address field shows it; %2$@ is what it wants: one or more of the prompt.kind strings, joined as a list by the system (in English: “your camera and your microphone”).</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_refuse" xml:space="preserve"><value>Don’t Allow</value><comment>Button in a tab&apos;s prompt: refuses what the page asked for, this time.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_says" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} says:</value><comment>The first line of the bar a tab shows when a page says something or asks (alert, confirm, prompt). %@ is the site, as the address field shows it; the page&apos;s own words follow on the next lines.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_sign_in" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sign in to {0}</value><comment>The first line of the bar a tab shows when a server asks for a name and a password before it answers (HTTP authentication). %@ is the server, as the address field shows a site: it is who gets what is typed.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_sign_in_again" xml:space="preserve"><value>That name or password was not accepted.</value><comment>In the sign-in bar, when the server refused what was sent before and asks again.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_sign_in_go" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sign In</value><comment>Button in the sign-in bar: sends the name and the password to the server that asked.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_sign_in_name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Name</value><comment>The sign-in bar&apos;s first field: the name to sign in with. Shown in the empty field and read as its label.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_sign_in_not_private" xml:space="preserve"><value>This connection is not private. What you type could be read by others.</value><comment>In the sign-in bar, when the server is not reached over https: the name and password travel unprotected.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_sign_in_password" xml:space="preserve"><value>Password</value><comment>The sign-in bar&apos;s second field. Shown in the empty field and read as its label.</comment></data>
  <data name="prompt_silence" xml:space="preserve"><value>Stop This Page’s Messages</value><comment>Button in the bar that shows what a page says, from the page&apos;s second message on: this page is shown no more messages until another page loads.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_change" xml:space="preserve"><value>Change…</value><comment>Button beside &apos;Searches go to …&apos; on an empty tab: opens the list of search engines.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_default" xml:space="preserve"><value>Default</value><comment>Said of the search engine that searches go to when nobody has chosen another, in the list of search engines at the first run.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_goes_to" xml:space="preserve"><value>Searches go to {0}.</value><comment>On an empty tab, under its hint. %@ is a search engine&apos;s own name, such as Ecosia.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_in_use" xml:space="preserve"><value>In use</value><comment>Said of the search engine that searches go to now, in the list of search engines when it is opened to change it.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_keep" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cancel</value><comment>Button that closes the list of search engines without changing where searches go. Not shown at the first run, when one has to be chosen.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_keep_default" xml:space="preserve"><value>Keep {0}</value><comment>Button under the list of search engines at the first run: leaves searches going to the default engine. %@ is that engine&apos;s own name, such as DuckDuckGo.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_question_detail" xml:space="preserve"><value>What you type in the address field that is not an address is sent to the search engine you choose. Nothing is sent until you search.</value><comment>Under the heading of the list of search engines.</comment></data>
  <data name="search_question_title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Where should your searches go?</value><comment>Heading of the first run&apos;s question, and of the same list when it is opened again: the person chooses a search engine.</comment></data>
  <data name="selection_search_with" xml:space="preserve"><value>Search with {0}</value><comment>In the menu of words that are selected in a page: search for those words, in a new tab. %@ is the search engine&apos;s own name, such as Ecosia.</comment></data>
  <data name="sites_asleep" xml:space="preserve"><value>Asleep</value><comment>Said of a tab that has no page loaded now, to a screen reader, with the tab&apos;s name: a Site that was closed, or a tab that came back after a quit. Opening it loads it.</comment></data>
